{% from "components/section-content.njk" import sectionContent %}

<section class="region">
	{% call sectionContent("about", "About", "flow text-box-trim-both") %}
		<!--origin story-->
		<p>
			In 2019, I was studying chartered accountancy when I watched my cousin
			design and build a website from scratch, writing code and seeing it take
			shape in real time. I knew I'd found the work I wanted to do. So I learned
			to build for the web.
		</p>

		<!--how I build-->
		<h3>How I Build</h3>
		<p>
			Since May 2020, I've designed and built websites for clients from the
			ground up, handling everything from prototyping in the browser to
			deployment. I write semantic HTML and resilient CSS that communicates the
			design intent to the browser, leaning on the cascade and intrinsic layouts
			instead of hardcoded breakpoints or magic numbers. The interface leverages
			the web platform (HTML, CSS, and JavaScript), with a framework on top only
			where it earns its place.
		</p>

		<!--why it matters-->
		<h3>Why I Build This Way</h3>
		<p>
			I build this way because it lasts, and because it includes more people.
			The platform outlasts every framework, so the HTML and CSS stay portable
			across frameworks and a change of stack doesn't mean a rewrite. What I
			ship stays accessible regardless of ability, device, browser, or
			connection speed.
		</p>
		<p>
			Performance is part of that, not a separate pass: I keep the payload
			small, and I treat a slow connection as a condition to support rather than
			an edge case to apologise for.
		</p>

		<!--what I'm looking for-->
		<h3>What I'm Looking For</h3>
		<p>
			I like working alongside developers and teams who see frontend the same
			way: a foundation worth building well, not a layer to rush past. I'm
			looking for a full-time role, based in Mumbai but open to relocation and
			to remote work.
		</p>
	{% endcall %}
</section>
